# Source for q/ae-chd-ph.js: Congenital, Pulmonary hypertension.
CH="Congenital"; PH="Pulmonary hypertension"
Q=[
# --- ASD / PFO ---
(CH,"The most common type of atrial septal defect is:","Secundum ASD, at the fossa ovalis",["Primum ASD","Sinus venosus ASD","Coronary sinus ASD"],"Secundum defects make up about 70% of ASDs and can often be closed with a catheter device.","F"),
(CH,"A primum ASD is located:","In the lower atrial septum, next to the AV valves",["At the fossa ovalis","Near the SVC entrance","In the coronary sinus roof"],"It is part of the AV canal spectrum and is usually associated with a cleft anterior mitral leaflet.","F"),
(CH,"A superior sinus venosus ASD is classically associated with:","Anomalous drainage of the right upper pulmonary vein",["A cleft mitral valve","Coarctation","Ebstein anomaly"],"It lies near the SVC entrance, high in the septum, and is easily missed on TTE.","F"),
(CH,"The best transthoracic view to see the atrial septum without false dropout is:","Subcostal four-chamber",["Apical four-chamber","Parasternal long axis","Suprasternal"],"In the apical view the thin septum is parallel to the beam and can look falsely absent.","F"),
(CH,"A significant left-to-right ASD shunt causes:","Right atrial and right ventricular volume overload",["LV hypertrophy","Left atrial dilation only","Aortic dilation"],"The RV dilates and the septum may move paradoxically from volume overload.","F"),
(CH,"Before device closure of a secundum ASD, TEE is used to assess:","Defect size and the rims of tissue around it",["Aortic valve area","LV mass","Coronary flow reserve"],"Adequate rims are needed to anchor the device. Large or deficient-rim defects may need surgery.","F"),
(CH,"A patent foramen ovale is:","A flap-like opening that failed to seal after birth, present in about a quarter of adults",["A hole in the ventricular septum","A type of primum ASD","Always associated with Down syndrome"],"It may allow paradoxical embolism. A bubble study with Valsalva detects it.","F"),
(CH,"Paradoxical embolism means:","A venous clot crossing a right-to-left shunt into the systemic circulation",["A clot from the LA appendage","A clot that dissolves","A vegetation on the pulmonic valve"],"It can cause stroke in patients with PFO or ASD.","F"),
(CH,"An unroofed coronary sinus ASD may be associated with:","Persistent left superior vena cava",["Bicuspid aortic valve","Marfan syndrome","HCM"],"The defect lets blood pass between the coronary sinus and the LA.","C"),
# --- VSD ---
(CH,"The most common type of ventricular septal defect is:","Perimembranous",["Muscular","Inlet","Outlet (supracristal)"],"It sits just below the aortic valve near the tricuspid septal leaflet, best seen in PSAX at about 10 o'clock.","F"),
(CH,"Small muscular VSDs in infants often:","Close spontaneously",["Always need surgery","Cause tamponade","Enlarge with age"],"Many trabecular (muscular) defects close in the first years of life.","F"),
(CH,"An outlet (supracristal) VSD is associated with:","Prolapse of the right coronary cusp causing aortic regurgitation",["Mitral stenosis","Ebstein anomaly","Coarctation only"],"The cusp loses support and herniates into the defect.","C"),
(CH,"A 'restrictive' VSD has:","A high-velocity jet and a large LV-to-RV pressure gradient",["Low-velocity flow and equal pressures","No flow","Right-to-left flow"],"Small defects keep RV pressure low. Large non-restrictive defects equalize pressures.","F"),
(CH,"Systolic BP is 120 mmHg and VSD peak velocity is 5 m/s. What is the estimated RV systolic pressure?","20 mmHg",["100 mmHg","80 mmHg","45 mmHg"],"LV-RV gradient = 4 × 5² = 100 mmHg. RVSP ≈ systolic BP − 100 = 20 mmHg (without LVOT obstruction).","C"),
(CH,"A large VSD with left-to-right shunt causes volume overload of the:","Left atrium and left ventricle",["Right atrium only","Right ventricle only","Aorta only"],"Shunted blood returns through the lungs to the left heart. ASDs overload the right heart instead.","C"),
(CH,"Eisenmenger syndrome is:","Reversal of a left-to-right shunt to right-to-left because of severe pulmonary vascular disease",["Closure of a VSD","A type of ASD","A bicuspid valve"],"Patients become cyanotic. Shunt closure is no longer possible.","F"),
(CH,"An inlet VSD is part of:","An atrioventricular canal (endocardial cushion) defect",["Tetralogy of Fallot","Transposition","Coarctation"],"It lies beneath the AV valves.","F"),
# --- PDA ---
(CH,"A patent ductus arteriosus connects the:","Descending aorta and the pulmonary artery",["Aorta and the RA","SVC and LA","LV and RV"],"In the fetus the ductus lets blood bypass the lungs. It normally closes within days of birth.","F"),
(CH,"Doppler in the main pulmonary artery with a PDA typically shows:","Continuous flow toward the transducer throughout the cardiac cycle",["Systolic flow only","No flow","Flow only in diastole away from the transducer"],"Aortic pressure exceeds PA pressure in both systole and diastole.","F"),
(CH,"A significant PDA causes enlargement of the:","Left atrium and left ventricle",["Right atrium","Right ventricle only","IVC"],"Extra blood flows through the lungs and returns to the left heart.","F"),
(CH,"The ductus arteriosus can be kept open in a newborn with:","Prostaglandin infusion",["Indomethacin","Beta-blockers","Aspirin only"],"Ductal-dependent lesions need it open. Indomethacin or ibuprofen are used to close it.","C"),
# --- AV canal ---
(CH,"A complete atrioventricular canal defect includes:","A primum ASD, an inlet VSD and a common AV valve",["A secundum ASD and PDA","Coarctation and bicuspid valve","Tetralogy features"],"It is strongly associated with Down syndrome.","F"),
(CH,"Atrioventricular canal defects are most strongly associated with:","Down syndrome (trisomy 21)",["Turner syndrome","Marfan syndrome","Williams syndrome"],"About 40% of children with Down syndrome have congenital heart disease, most often AV canal defects.","F"),
(CH,"In AV canal defects, the left and right AV valves:","Sit at the same level, losing the normal apical offset of the tricuspid valve",["Are normally offset","Are both bicuspid","Are absent"],"Loss of offset is a key clue in the A4C view.","C"),
# --- Tetralogy ---
(CH,"The four features of tetralogy of Fallot are:","VSD, overriding aorta, RVOT obstruction and RV hypertrophy",["ASD, PDA, coarctation and AS","VSD, PDA, MS and LVH","Bicuspid valve, coarctation, MR and LVH"],"It is the most common cyanotic congenital heart disease after infancy.","F"),
(CH,"In tetralogy of Fallot, the overriding aorta is best seen in:","Parasternal long axis, sitting over the VSD",["Subcostal IVC view","Suprasternal arch view","Apical two-chamber view"],"The aortic root straddles the septum, receiving blood from both ventricles.","F"),
(CH,"After tetralogy repair, a common long-term problem is:","Severe pulmonic regurgitation with RV dilation",["Mitral stenosis","Aortic stenosis","Coarctation"],"RV size and function are followed to time pulmonary valve replacement.","F"),
# --- Transposition and others ---
(CH,"In d-transposition of the great arteries:","The aorta arises from the RV and the pulmonary artery from the LV",["The aorta arises from the LV normally","Both arteries arise from the LV","There is only one great artery"],"The great arteries run parallel instead of crossing. Survival depends on mixing through an ASD, VSD or PDA.","F"),
(CH,"On echo, the great arteries in d-TGA appear:","Parallel, side by side",["Crossing normally","Absent","As a single trunk"],"Normally the RVOT crosses in front of the aorta.","F"),
(CH,"In congenitally corrected (l-) transposition, the:","Morphologic RV supports the systemic circulation",["Morphologic LV supports the systemic circulation","Aorta arises from the LV","Circulation is completely normal"],"Ventricles are inverted. The systemic tricuspid valve often leaks and heart block is common.","C"),
(CH,"Which feature identifies the morphologic right ventricle?","A moderator band and a more apical AV valve",["Smooth walls and two papillary muscles","Mitral-aortic fibrous continuity","An AV valve attached only to the free wall"],"The tricuspid valve has septal attachments and three leaflets.","F"),
(CH,"The morphologic right atrium is identified by:","Receiving the IVC and having a broad, triangular appendage",["A narrow finger-like appendage","Receiving the pulmonary veins","Having no appendage"],"The left atrial appendage is long and narrow.","C"),
(CH,"Truncus arteriosus is:","A single great artery arising over a VSD that supplies the aorta, pulmonary and coronary arteries",["Two great arteries with a PDA","A narrowed aorta","A type of ASD"],"It is a cyanotic lesion needing surgery in infancy.","C"),
(CH,"Total anomalous pulmonary venous return means:","All pulmonary veins drain into the right side of the heart or systemic veins",["Pulmonary veins drain normally into the LA","Only one vein is narrowed","The SVC drains into the LA"],"An ASD is needed for survival. The LA looks small with no veins entering it.","C"),
(CH,"Scimitar syndrome is:","Partial anomalous drainage of right lung veins to the IVC",["A form of coarctation","A double aortic arch","Tricuspid atresia"],"The anomalous vein looks like a curved sword on chest X-ray.","C"),
(CH,"Cor triatriatum sinister is:","A membrane dividing the left atrium into two chambers",["A double-chambered RV","Three atria from birth defects of the RA","Tricuspid atresia"],"It can obstruct pulmonary venous inflow, mimicking mitral stenosis.","C"),
(CH,"A parachute mitral valve has:","All chordae attached to a single papillary muscle",["Two normal papillary muscles","A cleft anterior leaflet","Three leaflets"],"It causes mitral stenosis and is part of Shone complex.","C"),
(CH,"Shone complex is a series of left-sided obstructions that may include:","Supramitral ring, parachute mitral valve, subaortic stenosis and coarctation",["ASD, VSD and PDA","Tetralogy and transposition","Ebstein anomaly and PFO"],"Look for every level of obstruction from LA to aorta.","C"),
(CH,"A Fontan circulation:","Routes systemic venous blood directly to the pulmonary arteries without a pumping ventricle",["Adds a second LV","Closes an ASD","Replaces the aortic valve"],"Used for single-ventricle hearts such as tricuspid atresia or hypoplastic left heart. Flow to the lungs is passive.","C"),
(CH,"Hypoplastic left heart syndrome includes:","A very small LV, mitral and aortic atresia or stenosis, and a hypoplastic aorta",["A large LV","Isolated pulmonic stenosis","A secundum ASD only"],"It is treated with staged surgery leading to a Fontan circulation.","C"),
(CH,"Classic cyanotic congenital lesions (the 'five Ts') include tetralogy, transposition, truncus, tricuspid atresia and:","Total anomalous pulmonary venous return",["Secundum ASD","Small PDA","Bicuspid valve"],"Most acyanotic lesions are left-to-right shunts or obstructions.","F"),
(CH,"Qp/Qs is calculated as:","(RVOT area × RVOT VTI) ÷ (LVOT area × LVOT VTI)",["LVOT VTI ÷ RVOT VTI only","TR velocity ÷ MR velocity","PA pressure ÷ aortic pressure"],"A ratio of 1.5 or more suggests a significant left-to-right shunt.","F"),
(CH,"RVOT diameter 2.4 cm and LVOT diameter 2.0 cm, with equal VTIs of 20 cm. What is Qp/Qs?","About 1.4",["About 1.0","About 2.4","About 0.7"],"Area ratio = 2.4² ÷ 2.0² = 5.76 ÷ 4 = 1.44 with equal VTIs.","C"),
(CH,"Double-chambered right ventricle is:","Division of the RV by anomalous muscle bundles, causing mid-cavity obstruction",["A double outlet RV","A second LV","Ebstein anomaly"],"It is often associated with a perimembranous VSD.","C"),
(CH,"Dextrocardia means:","The heart lies in the right chest with its apex pointing right",["The heart is enlarged","The aorta is on the right","The RV is enlarged"],"Imaging windows must be mirrored on the right chest.","F"),
(CH,"A bicuspid aortic valve, coarctation and Turner syndrome are linked; Turner syndrome affects:","Females with a missing or partial X chromosome",["Males with an extra X chromosome","Both sexes with trisomy 21","Only adults over 60"],"Screen these patients for aortic dilation and coarctation.","F"),
(CH,"After device closure of an ASD, echo checks for:","Residual shunt, device position, effusion and nearby valve function",["Aortic stenosis only","LV hypertrophy only","Coronary origins only"],"Erosion of the device into the aorta or pericardium is a rare serious complication.","F"),
(CH,"In an adult with an unexplained dilated RV, the sonographer should look for:","An ASD, especially sinus venosus, or anomalous pulmonary venous return",["A bicuspid aortic valve","Mitral stenosis only","An LV apical thrombus"],"Shunts at atrial level overload the right heart and are easy to miss on TTE.","F"),
# --- Pulmonary hypertension ---
(PH,"Pulmonary hypertension is defined by right heart catheterization as a mean PA pressure greater than:","20 mmHg at rest",["10 mmHg","50 mmHg","100 mmHg"],"The 2022 ESC/ERS guidelines lowered the threshold from 25 to 20 mmHg.","F"),
(PH,"A peak TR velocity of more than 3.4 m/s indicates:","High echo probability of pulmonary hypertension",["Low probability","Normal pressure","Tricuspid stenosis"],"2.8 m/s or less is low probability; 2.9 to 3.4 m/s is intermediate and depends on other signs.","F"),
(PH,"PA systolic pressure is estimated from:","4 × (peak TR velocity)² + RA pressure",["4 × (peak MR velocity)²","LVOT VTI × area","Peak AR velocity only"],"This assumes no pulmonic stenosis or RVOT obstruction.","F"),
(PH,"The most common cause of pulmonary hypertension is:","Left heart disease (WHO group 2)",["Idiopathic PAH","Chronic thromboembolic disease","Sickle cell disease"],"Raised LA pressure from LV dysfunction or valve disease is transmitted back to the lungs.","F"),
(PH,"WHO group 3 pulmonary hypertension is caused by:","Lung disease or chronic hypoxia",["Left heart disease","Chronic thromboembolism","Idiopathic arterial disease"],"COPD, interstitial lung disease and sleep apnea are examples.","F"),
(PH,"WHO group 4 pulmonary hypertension is:","Chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension (CTEPH)",["Left heart disease","Lung disease","Idiopathic PAH"],"Unresolved clots narrow the pulmonary arteries. It can sometimes be cured surgically.","F"),
(PH,"A D-shaped LV in systole in the PSAX view indicates:","RV pressure overload",["RV volume overload only","Normal heart","LV hypertrophy"],"High RV pressure flattens the septum in systole. Flattening mainly in diastole suggests volume overload.","F"),
(PH,"Septal flattening mainly in diastole suggests:","RV volume overload, such as from an ASD or severe TR",["RV pressure overload only","Normal heart","Aortic stenosis"],"In pressure overload, flattening is greatest at end-systole.","F"),
(PH,"An eccentricity index greater than 1.1 means:","The LV is D-shaped from septal flattening",["The LV is perfectly round","The RV is small","The aorta is dilated"],"It is the ratio of two perpendicular LV diameters in PSAX.","C"),
(PH,"An RVOT acceleration time shorter than 105 ms suggests:","Pulmonary hypertension",["Normal pulmonary pressure","Pulmonic stenosis","Low cardiac output"],"High pulmonary resistance causes the PW signal to peak early.","F"),
(PH,"Mid-systolic notching of the RVOT PW Doppler signal suggests:","High pulmonary vascular resistance",["Pulmonic regurgitation","Normal findings","An ASD"],"Reflected pressure waves from stiff pulmonary arteries cause the notch.","C"),
(PH,"An RV to LV basal diameter ratio greater than 1.0 in the apical four-chamber view suggests:","RV enlargement, supporting pulmonary hypertension",["Normal RV","LV dilation","Small RV"],"Normally the RV is about two-thirds the size of the LV.","F"),
(PH,"A main pulmonary artery diameter greater than about 25 mm suggests:","Pulmonary artery dilation, supporting pulmonary hypertension",["Normal size","Pulmonic stenosis","Coarctation"],"It is one of the supporting signs in the echo probability assessment.","F"),
(PH,"A pericardial effusion in a patient with pulmonary arterial hypertension indicates:","A poor prognosis",["A good prognosis","A normal finding","An unrelated infection"],"It reflects high right atrial pressure and right heart failure.","F"),
(PH,"The RV free wall thickness considered hypertrophied is more than about:","5 mm",["1 mm","15 mm","25 mm"],"RV hypertrophy develops with chronic pressure overload.","F"),
(PH,"Mean PA pressure can be estimated from:","4 × (early diastolic PR peak velocity)² + RA pressure",["4 × (end-diastolic PR velocity)² only","The TR VTI","The RVOT diameter"],"End-diastolic PR velocity estimates PA diastolic pressure.","C"),
(PH,"McConnell's sign in acute pulmonary embolism is:","RV free wall akinesis with preserved motion of the RV apex",["A thickened RV with good function","LV apical ballooning","Pericardial effusion"],"It is suggestive but not specific; it can occur in RV infarction.","F"),
(PH,"The '60/60 sign' in acute pulmonary embolism refers to:","RVOT acceleration time under 60 ms with a TR gradient under 60 mmHg",["Heart rate over 60 and EF over 60%","TAPSE over 60 mm","Mitral E over 60 cm/s"],"An acutely loaded RV can't generate high pressures, so the TR gradient stays modest despite high resistance.","C"),
(PH,"In acute massive pulmonary embolism, the TR velocity is often only moderately raised because:","A normal, thin-walled RV can't generate very high pressures suddenly",["The RV is hypertrophied","The TR jet disappears","PA pressure falls"],"Very high TR velocity suggests chronic pulmonary hypertension with a hypertrophied RV.","C"),
(PH,"A thrombus seen moving through the right atrium ('clot in transit') indicates:","A high risk of pulmonary embolism, needing urgent treatment",["A benign normal variant","A Chiari network","A myxoma"],"Report it to the physician immediately.","F"),
(PH,"Pulmonary arterial hypertension (group 1) includes:","Idiopathic PAH, PAH from connective tissue disease, drugs and congenital shunts",["PH from left heart failure","PH from COPD","PH from chronic clots"],"Scleroderma and appetite-suppressant drugs are recognized causes.","F"),
(PH,"The TAPSE to PA systolic pressure ratio is used to assess:","How well the RV copes with its afterload (RV-PA coupling)",["Aortic stenosis severity","LV diastolic function","Mitral valve area"],"A low ratio (under about 0.55 mm/mmHg) suggests poor coupling and a worse outlook.","C"),
(PH,"Right atrial area greater than about 18 cm² in an adult suggests:","Right atrial enlargement, supporting raised right-sided pressure",["Normal RA size","LA enlargement","Pericardial effusion"],"RA size is measured at end-systole in an apical four-chamber view.","F"),
(PH,"In pulmonary hypertension, the IVC is often:","Dilated with poor collapse, reflecting high RA pressure",["Small and fully collapsing","Absent","Unchanged in all patients"],"RA pressure estimated from the IVC is added to the TR gradient.","F"),
(PH,"If TR is trivial and the signal is too weak to measure, the sonographer can:","Use agitated saline to enhance the TR Doppler signal",["Guess the RVSP","Measure the MR jet instead","Use PW Doppler only"],"An incomplete envelope underestimates pressure.","F"),
(PH,"Pulmonic stenosis must be excluded before using TR velocity to estimate PA pressure because:","TR velocity reflects RV pressure, which exceeds PA pressure when PS is present",["PS lowers TR velocity","PS removes TR","PS has no effect"],"With PS, PA systolic pressure = RVSP − the pulmonic valve gradient.","C"),
]
